a different row width, the hash values actually differ—so precomputation won't help much. I'm trying to figure out what fraction of the \"STARK excl trace\" time is actual GPU kernel execution versus CPU overhead and memory transfers. The most promising optimization seems to be batching the Round 0 kernels for small AIRs into a single unified approach, though previous attempts ran into Rust optimizer issues and out-of-memory problems.\n\nLooking at the failed batch-round0-caller-routing attempt, the infrastructure is already in place but inactive because of OOM in the GKR phase. The code exists and shows no regression when disabled (Round 0 stays at 178ms), so the question is whether that OOM issue has been resolved since then.\n\nChecking the subsequent tasks, they tackled separate optimizations but didn't address the batched path activation. The OOM was a pre-existing issue from a CUDA rebuild that increased memory overhead, not something caused by the batching approach itself. If that's been fixed, the batched path could potentially be turned back on.\n\nNow looking at overlapping compute and memory operations in the Trace Commit phase — the poseidon2 hashing and merkle tree building are sequential right now, but I could pipeline hashing one segment's traces while constructing the merkle tree for another.\n\nThere's also the H2D copy overhead: 30,035 copies totaling 3.7GB, mostly tiny transfers. Static data like interaction descriptors and constraint metadata gets copied repeatedly across segments and rounds, so caching that on GPU could eliminate thousands of these copies. The 331ms spent on H2D operations is spread across all phases, but reducing the small copies within STARK exclusion trace could still be meaningful.\n\nLooking at the component breakdown, LogUp GKR dominates at 38.2% of the STARK time, scaling 1.98x from APC 0 to 300, while Trace Commit is 18.2% and scales 2.13x. Round 0 and the MLE rounds show sublinear scaling, suggesting they might be memor