iminishing returns) or optimizing memory access patterns and shared memory usage. The Round 0 zerocheck and logup kernels are also significant contributors at 255ms and 235ms respectively, each running on 8 threads with similar utilization patterns.\n\nFor the GKR input eval kernel specifically, I'm examining how it evaluates interaction polynomials at a given point for each trace row — each thread processes one row and reads trace column values, interaction descriptors defining the DAG structure, and partition data. Given that grid sizing isn't the bottleneck and pre-allocation eliminated mutex contention, the kernel is likely hitting memory bandwidth limits when dealing with large AIRs that have many columns.\n\nThe core issue is that memory access patterns might be inefficient — if the trace is stored in column-major format, accessing multiple columns per row requires strided reads that hurt cache performance. Rearranging to row-major or block layout could help, but that would require restructuring the entire matrix layout, which seems impractical. A better angle might be reducing the actual work instead of just optimizing how it's parallelized.\n\nLooking at the APC 300 case, interaction messages drop significantly but GPU time for GKR kernels doesn't scale proportionally because there are more AIRs to process (623 vs 99), each with fixed per-AIR overhead and potentially more complex DAG structures. I'm wondering if we could skip GKR entirely for AIRs that have no interactions, which could eliminate unnecessary kernel launches.\n\nThere's also the descriptor-array batching approach that was already implemented for Round 0 — we could apply that same pattern to GKR input evaluation. Instead of launching a separate kernel for each AIR, we'd batch multiple small AIRs into a single kernel call, reducing launches from 486 down to roughly 8 (one per thread), eliminating per-AIR DAG setup overhead, and improving GPU utilization by consolidating many small sequential kernels into fewer large ones.\n\nThe challenge is that each AIR has its own DAG structure, so encoding that into a descriptor array gets complicated. Let me look at what's actually happening in the current code path. The previous optimizations already extracted per-AIR work into a standalone function and eliminated mutex contention through pre-allocation, so I need to understand where the real overhead is now. Looking at the rightsize report, segment 0 takes 242ms and segment 1 takes 62ms — the asymmetry suggests segment 0 with its larger AIR set is the bottleneck. The total GKR input eval is about 304ms wall time, and the kernel GPU time across all 486 instances is 456ms, which would be roughly 57ms per thread if evenly distributed.\n\nBut the actual wall time for segment 0 is 242ms, indicating severe load imbalance across threads. The pre-allocation report shows segment 1improved dramatically (4.15x) because allocation overhead dominated there, while segment 0 only improved 1.07x since it's kernel-bound with fewer but much larger AIRs. Segment 0 has around 38 AIRs per thread at APC 300, and if a few large AIRs each take 10-20ms, whichever thread gets the most large ones becomes the bottleneck. The height-sorted round-robin distribution should help mitigate this imbalance.\n\nThe current approach doesn't account for per-AIR fixed costs or how height scales nonlinearly with kernel time. I'm wondering if we could split large AIRs across multiple threads instead of having one thread process an entire AIR — that would require modifying the kernel to handle partial row ranges and then combining results. That's essentially what the rightsize attempt was trying to do, but the bottleneck wasn't SM saturation so it didn't help. Another possibility: what if we processed the GKR input evaluation for all segments simultaneously instead of sequentially? That would give us 623 combined work items across 8 threads, distributing the large AIRs more evenly. Though I need to think about whether there's a dependency reason they're processed sequentially — each segment produces separate fractions that feed into the GKR protocol.\n\nLooking at the actual code flow and the metrics, the GKR input eval runs per segment sequentially (seg0 takes 242ms, seg1 takes 62ms). If we could run both segments' evaluations concurrently, we'd be combining 623 work items across 8 threads for better load distribution.\n\nThe key question is whether the two segments are independent for GKR input eval—each one computes fractional polynomial values based on its own trace data and the interaction structure, using the same evaluation point from the transcript. If they're truly independent, concurrent processing could save around 50ms, though thread imbalance from large AIRs would limit the actual gains.\n\nLooking at the actual numbers, seg0 has 306 work items taking 242ms while seg1 has 317 items taking only 62ms. The problem is that seg0's few massive AIRs dominate their threads, so even combining the workloads wouldn't help much since those large items can't be parallelized further. I should step back and think about a different optimization strategy entirely.\n\nWhat if I pipeline the phases across segments instead of optimizing within a single phase? Rather than processing each segment sequentially through all phases, I could start the next segment's work while the current one is still running through later stages.\n\nI need to verify the actual execution order—whether it's truly sequential per segment or if phases already run across all segments together. Let me check the prover structure to understand the dependency chain.\n\nLooking at the metric hierarchy, Trace Commit sits as a sibling to Constraints and Openings under STARK, which suggests it processes all segment traces together as a stacked matrix rather than per-segment. So the flow is: all traces get committed together, then Round 0 and MLE rounds execute across segments, followed by LogUp GKR and the opening proofs.\n\nNow I'm focusing on concrete, actionable improvements. My strongest candidate is overlapping the Trace Commit hashing work across multiple CUDA streams—specifically the poseidon2 hashing that dominates the 246ms Trace Commit time. If I can split the RS-coded matrix into chunks and process each on a separate stream, I should be able to hide some of that latency.\n\nAnother promising angle is merging the GKR input evaluation segments. Right now seg0 and seg1 run sequentially for 304ms total, but if I collect work items from both and dispatch them together across the thread pool, I can improve load balancing and save 30-50ms.\n\nThe third idea involves reducing CPU overhead in the GKR protocol itself.  The protocol runs 14+ rounds with CPU sampling, totaling around 215ms. I could batch multiple kernel launches before sampling and use asynchronous copies with a separate compute stream to minimize gaps between GPU rounds. This is riskier due to sequential dependencies in the protocol, but could yield 20-40ms.\n\nFinally, there's the poseidon2 compression layer—457 launches that collectively take 12ms but carry significant launch overhead. Batching these launches instead of running them sequentially should be straightforward and save 5-10ms.\n\nI'm also considering whether I can interleave the GKR input evaluation with the protocol itself using multiple streams. If I run the protocol for segment 0 on one stream while evaluating segment 1 on others, I might overlap about 50-60ms of work. The risk is that segments need to be combined for the protocol to work correctly.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the merged batch approach for GKR input eval. Instead of processing segments sequentially, I could combine all 623 work items into a single batch and distribute them across 8 threads using height-sorted round-robin scheduling.\n\nThe current sequential approach takes 304ms total (242ms for seg0 + 62ms for seg1). With merging, the large seg0 AIRs would be mixed with