rk and keep the GPU busier. streams\n2. Overlap the per-trace fold operations across streams\n3. Reduce the CPU-GPU gap between operations\n\nThe batched evaluation kernels (zerocheck_batch_mle, logup_batch_mle, etc.) would remain on a single stream since they already batch all traces.\n\nStructure:\n```\nfor round:\n    // Phase 1: CPU-only metadata (single thread, no change)\n    collect_metadata()\n    \n    // Phase 2: Batched interpolation (single stream, no change)\n    batched_interpolate_columns()\n    \n    // Phase 3: Per-trace prep (MULTI-STREAM NEW)\n    parallel_for trace in traces:\n        build_trace_ctx()\n        precompute_logup_combinations()\n    sync_all_streams()\n    \n    // Phase 4: Batched evaluation (single stream, no change)\n    evaluate_zerocheck_batched()\n    evaluate_logup_batched()\n    \n    // Phase 5: PLE fold (MULTI-STREAM NEW)\n    parallel_for trace in traces:\n        fold_ple_from_evals()\n    sync_all_streams()\n    \n    // Phase 6: Sample challenge (CPU, single thread)\n    sample_challenge()\n```\n\nThe performance gains come from parallelizing the logup combination precomputation and the PLE fold operations across multiple threads, which should cut about 50ms at APC 300 and roughly 10ms at APC 0 where there's less parallelization opportunity.\n\nWith these savings, the PC 300 STARK time drops from 1349ms to 1299ms, and APC 0 goes from 2138ms to 2128ms, improving the ratio from 1.59x to 1.64x. It's a modest improvement, and the implementation adds complexity around buffer management and stream synchronization, so I need to weigh whether it's worth the effort. Let me reconsider the actual savings by looking more carefully at what's already batched versus what has per-trace overhead. 8-thread multi-streaming: ~10ms. Savings: ~67ms.\n\nBut the fold operations (~18ms) also need to happen after the batched eval, not during. So:\n- Per-trace prep overhead: ~59ms → ~8ms (savings: 51ms)\n- Fold overhead: ~18ms → ~3ms (savings: 