{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VkGcrgzHFTwgfusn9AvibL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"OK, now I see what's happening. The `LogupMleBatchBuilder::new()` creates per-trace `DeviceBuffer` allocations (line 324: `DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(intermediates_len)`) for each trace's intermediates. These go through the `MEMORY_MANAGER` mutex, causing per-trace allocation overhead.\n\nBut looking at the numbers:\n- At APC 300, there are ~310 traces per MLE round with interactions\n- Each trace gets a DeviceBuffer allocation via `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity`\n- This triggers `cudaMallocAsync` through the memory manager\n\n\n- With the pool allocator, each call is ~1-5μs, so 310 calls × ~3μs adds up to roughly 1ms per MLE round per batch, which over 16 rounds comes to about 16ms\n\nThat's a modest overhead but worth investigating further. I'm noticing the nsys data shows 791 instances of `precompute_logup_combinations_kernel`, but I can't find where this function is being called in the `LogupMleBatchBuilder` or `evaluate_logup_batched` code, so I need to track down its source.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1828,"cache_read_input_tokens":151708,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1828},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c275100b-138e-43b1-b6e9-07f6d601a462","uuid":"9d78e799-33e5-4fc6-b11a-1cc98c6d5deb"}
